# Weather Monitoring Modular System (Frontend)

A real-time IoT weather monitoring dashboard built with [Dash](https://dash.plotly.com/) / Plotly.
In the repository this application is saved as **`app.py`**; on the Raspberry Pi it runs as
`weather_app.py`. The two are the same application.

The dashboard **only reads** from a TimescaleDB (PostgreSQL) database. It never writes sensor
data — readings are produced by the pipeline (`data_pipeline.py`) and stored by
`live_capture.py`.

## Features

- **Overview** — status banner per node plus the four derived metric cards:
  heat index, dew point, absolute humidity and AQI (US EPA).
- **Stations / Map** — Plotly scatter-map of every node with a location.
  Online nodes are green and larger; offline nodes are dimmed. Nodes with no
  lat/lon in their readings are omitted.
- **Trends** — time-series charts of any calculation or sensor, with a
  24 h / 168 h / 720 h range filter.
- **Devices** — total / online / offline counts and a device table with
  ONLINE status pill, WiFi / MQTT connection pills and last-message time.
- **Alerts** — filterable alert/event list (currently an empty state until an
  alert source exists).
- **Account** — "signed in as" details and a change-password form.
- **Authentication** — login page backed by `auth.py`; the username is the
  student number and passwords are stored **hashed (PBKDF2)** in the same
  database. No dashboard data is rendered to a signed-out browser.

## Requirements

- Python 3.9+
- A running TimescaleDB (or MySQL/Influx/other adapter if `config.get_db()` is
  pointed at one) containing at least:
  - a `readings` table (hypertable) with columns `node_id`, `time` and the
    sensor/calculation columns the dashboard queries, and
  - a `users` table for logins.
- Dependencies:

```bash
pip install dash plotly psycopg2-binary werkzeug
```

`requirements.txt` in this repository already lists everything (including
`paho-mqtt`, which only the pipeline needs).

## Quick start

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt

# point the app at the database (see config.py — the password is required)
export TS_HOST=localhost
export TS_PORT=5432
export TS_DB=iot_trust
export TS_USER=iot_user
export TS_PASSWORD=your_password

python app.py            # or: python weather_app.py
```

Open <http://localhost:5000> (or `http://<server-ip>:5000` from any browser).

## Environment variables

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `TS_HOST` | `localhost` | Database host (read by `config.py`) |
| `TS_PORT` | `5432` | Database port |
| `TS_DB` | `iot_trust` | Database name |
| `TS_USER` | `iot_user` | Database user |
| `TS_PASSWORD` | *(required)* | Database password |
| `DB_CHOICE` | `timescale` | Backend label shown in the sidebar |
| `LOOKBACK_HOURS` | `24` | How far back history queries go |
| `OFFLINE_AFTER_S` | `60` | Seconds without a reading before a node is "offline" |
| `REFRESH_MS` | `5000` | Dashboard auto-refresh interval (ms) |
| `CACHE_TTL_S` | `2` | Server-side DB query throttle (s) |
| `PORT` | `5000` | Dash server port |
| `SECRET_KEY` | generated | Session signing key; set it so sessions survive restarts (`auth.py`) |

## Student logins

Run `seed_users.py` once to load the class list into the `users` table
(it only inserts accounts that don't exist):

```bash
python seed_users.py            # add missing accounts
python seed_users.py --show     # list accounts + whether they changed their password
```

- Username: the student number (e.g. `444444`).
- First password: `$XY<student_number>&` (e.g. `$XY444444&`).
- Students change their own password under **Account** (needs the current
  password, minimum 8 characters).
- `--reset-passwords` puts everyone back on the default.

## Project structure (files the frontend depends on)

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `app.py` / `weather_app.py` | The Dash application: layout, routing, pages, callbacks |
| `auth.py` | Login accounts, hashing, session helpers |
| `config.py` | DB connection config; `get_db()` returns the adapter |
| `timescale_adapter.py` | `readings` schema, insert/query methods the dashboard calls |
| `seed_users.py` | Loads `class_list.csv` into `users` |

## Notes / known behaviour

- Every page callback checks the session; signed-out requests receive the login
  card and **no** readings, node names or derived values.
- The Map page plots a node only when it has `lat`/`lon` (from its latest
  reading). If the database stores none, the map shows an empty state.
- WiFi / MQTT pills show "—" when the schema has no `wifi`/`rssi` columns —
  those normally live on the ESP node's `diagnostics` topic, which is not yet
  ingested.
- The Alerts page returns an empty state until a real alert source exists.
